\section{A linear example: plant growth and nitrogen}
\label{sec:linear}

The loop is best shown in practice. This first example is deliberately simple and can be reproduced directly; a fully commented \textsf{R} script containing all the code and figures below is available in the Supplement.

\subsection{Step 1: from a verbal hypothesis to a generative model}

Every study starts with a question. Here: what is the effect of nitrogen fertilisation on plant growth? We plan to grow sunflowers across a gradient of nitrogen concentrations and harvest biomass after one season. The response $y$ is biomass, and the verbal hypothesis is that plants grow more when nitrogen increases.

That sentence is not yet something we can simulate from. To simulate, we must commit to three things it leaves open: the \emph{shape} of the relationship, the \emph{size} of the effect, and its \emph{consistency}. Assuming a positive linear relationship between biomass and nitrogen concentration $x$ gives

\begin{equation}
	y_i = a + b\,x_i + \varepsilon_i, \qquad \varepsilon_i \sim \mathrm{N}(0, \sigma)
	\label{eq:linear}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $a$ is the intercept, $b$ is the slope---the expected biomass increase per unit nitrogen, and our estimand---and $\sigma$ captures variability around the mean relationship. We now have to say what we think these parameters are:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# Our assumptions about the system, stated as numbers
	a     <- 30   # intercept: expected biomass (g) at zero nitrogen
	b     <-  7   # slope: biomass gain (g) per unit nitrogen; the estimand
	sigma <-  5   # residual SD (g): process variation plus measurement error
\end{lstlisting}

These values are not truth. They are assumptions, made explicit so that they can be argued with, and everything that follows is conditional on them. That is the point rather than a limitation: it moves the debate from the sample size to the reasoning behind it.

\subsection{Step 2: simulate the data the model implies}

We choose a design and generate data through the model structure: draw predictor values as the design specifies, compute the expected response, then add noise.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# Choose a design and simulate one study from the model above
	n <- 50                                          # number of plants
	x <- rnorm(n, mean = 10, sd = 4)                 # planned nitrogen gradient
	y <- a + b * x + rnorm(n, mean = 0, sd = sigma)  # expected response, plus noise
	fake <- data.frame(x = x, y = y)
\end{lstlisting}

In an experiment with fixed treatment levels rather than a gradient, only the line generating \texttt{x} changes, for example \texttt{x <- rep(c(0, 5, 10, 15, 20), each = 10)}. The rest of the loop is identical.

Plotting the result (Fig.~\ref{fig:fakedata}a) shows the hypothesis in the same visual form as a real results figure. The question to ask is biological rather than statistical: could sunflowers in this system plausibly produce these data? If the scatter looks far too tight, or the biomass values are impossible, then the assumptions are wrong, and it is very much cheaper to discover that now than after a field season.

We can also fit the model we intend to use and check that it recovers what we put in (Fig.~\ref{fig:fakedata}b):

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# Fit the planned analysis and look at the interval, not the p-value
	fit_lm <- lm(y ~ x, data = fake)
	confint(fit_lm)["x", ]      # 95% interval for the slope b
\end{lstlisting}

At this point it is tempting to look at the $p$-value. We deliberately do not. The quantity we carry forward is the \emph{width} of the interval for $b$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:fakedata}c). A narrow interval means the study pinned the nitrogen effect down; a wide one means the data are compatible with many different effect sizes, whatever the $p$-value says. Note also that this is a single study. Run it again and the interval shifts and changes length.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	% \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_linear_step2}
	\caption{Step 2 of the loop for the linear example. (a) Data simulated from Equation~\ref{eq:linear} with $a = 30$, $b = 7$, $\sigma = 5$ and $n = 50$: the hypothesis in visual form, showing effect size and scatter together. (b) The fitted model (solid) against the generating model (dashed). (c) The estimated slope and its 95\% interval. The length of this bar is the quantity the rest of the section is about.}
	\label{fig:fakedata}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Step 3: how precision responds to design}

We now play the planned study through many times. One run generates a dataset, fits the intended model, and records the interval width for $b$:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# One simulated study: generate data, fit the model, return the interval width
	one_run <- function(n, a, b, sigma, mean_x = 10, sd_x = 4) {
		x  <- rnorm(n, mean = mean_x, sd = sd_x)
		y  <- a + b * x + rnorm(n, 0, sigma)
		ci <- confint(lm(y ~ x))["x", ]
		unname(ci[2] - ci[1])
	}
	
	# Repeat the same study many times to see what this design typically delivers
	widths <- replicate(500, one_run(50, a, b, sigma))
\end{lstlisting}

Repeating this across sample sizes traces a design-precision curve (Fig.~\ref{fig:precision}a). Precision improves with $n$, but with sharply diminishing returns: interval width falls roughly as $1/\sqrt{n}$, so halving the interval costs four times the sample size. The figure carries two lines because a design does not have a precision but a distribution of precisions (Fig.~\ref{fig:precision}b), and the 80th percentile is the honest summary of what a single realised study is likely to achieve.

This particular case has a closed-form answer, $\mathrm{se}(b) = \sigma / (s_x\sqrt{n-1})$, and the simulation reproduces it exactly (Fig.~\ref{fig:precision}c). We show this deliberately: simulation was not necessary here, and a formula would have done. Building the machinery where it can be checked is what licenses using it in Sections~\ref{sec:nonlinear} and \ref{sec:limits}, where no formula exists.

Sample size is not the only lever, and often not the best one. Holding $n = 50$ fixed and varying only the spread of nitrogen values shrinks the interval substantially (Fig.~\ref{fig:precision}d):

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# Same n, wider nitrogen gradient: how much does spreading x out buy us?
	sd_x_values <- c(1, 2, 4, 6, 8)
	sapply(sd_x_values, function(s)
	mean(replicate(500, one_run(50, a, b, sigma, sd_x = s))))
\end{lstlisting}

This lever is essentially free, since it costs no more to fertilise across a wider range, and it is invisible to a power calculation that treats $n$ as the only design variable. Measurement quality is a third lever (Fig.~\ref{fig:precision}e): doubling $\sigma$ costs roughly a fourfold increase in $n$ to recover the same precision, so a better instrument or a repeated reading may be far cheaper than more plants.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	% \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_precision}
	\caption{Step 3: how the precision of the nitrogen effect responds to design choices. (a) Design-precision curve: mean and 80th percentile of the 95\% interval width against sample size. (b) At a single design ($n = 50$) the width is itself a random variable; the mean is a poor target because about half of realised studies fall beyond it. (c) The simulated curve reproduces the closed-form result, validating the machinery before it is used where no formula exists. (d) At fixed $n = 50$, spreading the predictor more widely improves precision markedly. (e) Doubling the residual SD costs roughly a fourfold increase in sample size.}
	\label{fig:precision}
\end{figure}

\subsection{From a scientific requirement to a sample size}

Applying the rule from Section~\ref{sec:loop}: suppose a fertiliser recommendation requires the nitrogen effect to within $\pm 0.25$\,g biomass per unit nitrogen. The design target is then an interval of width $0.5$, read off the curve at the 80th percentile (Fig.~\ref{fig:target}).

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# What sample size meets a target width of 0.5 in 80% of studies?
	n_grid <- seq(20, 300, by = 20)
	q80    <- sapply(n_grid, function(nn)
	quantile(replicate(500, one_run(nn, a, b, sigma)), 0.8))
	n_grid[which(q80 <= 0.5)[1]]
\end{lstlisting}

Under the assumptions above this gives $n \approx 120$. The number is conditional on the assumed $\sigma$ and predictor range, which is a feature rather than a limitation: it makes the basis of the design auditable. A reviewer who disagrees can argue with $\sigma$, a statement about the system, rather than with the sample size, which is merely its consequence.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	% \includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{fig_design_target}
	\caption{Choosing a sample size from a precision requirement. The curve shows the 80th percentile of the 95\% interval width for $b$; the horizontal line is the target width of $0.5$ implied by a requirement of $\pm 0.25$. The two meet at $n \approx 120$.}
	\label{fig:target}
\end{figure}

\subsection{What an imprecise design would do to our conclusions}

Everything so far concerns precision. The same machinery also reveals what poor precision does to \emph{interpretation}, which is where the consequences for the published literature lie.

Consider a more typical ecological situation than our flagship example: a small true effect ($b = 0.5$), noisy measurement ($\sigma = 15$) and a modest sample ($n = 20$). Simulating this design and applying a conventional significance filter gives a power of about 9\%. That much a power analysis would have told us. What it would not have told us is the rest. Conditional on reaching $p < 0.05$, this design reports an effect roughly four times too large, and carries the wrong sign about 7\% of the time \citep{gelmanPowerCalculationsAssessing2014}. The distribution of estimates that would be published barely overlaps the truth (Fig.~\ref{fig:typesm}).

The design therefore does not merely fail to detect the effect; it manufactures misleading results. This cannot be diagnosed after the fact from a single dataset, because the exaggeration is invisible from inside the study that produced it. It is, however, entirely visible beforehand, from the design alone.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	% \includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{fig_type_sm}
	\caption{The distribution of slope estimates from 4000 simulated studies with $b = 0.5$, $\sigma = 15$ and $n = 20$ (grey), and the subset that would reach $p < 0.05$ and so be most likely to be reported (dark). Conditional on significance, the design exaggerates the effect roughly fourfold and returns the wrong sign about 7\% of the time. The dashed line is the true value.}
	\label{fig:typesm}
\end{figure}



\section{A non-linear example: a saturating response to nitrogen}
\label{sec:nonlinear}

Many ecological relationships are non-linear: responses saturate, show thresholds, or peak at intermediate conditions. Simulation earns its place here, for two reasons. There is no closed-form expression for the precision of a non-linear parameter, so the analytic shortcut used in Section~\ref{sec:linear} is unavailable. And the same data can often be explained by quite different parameter combinations, which makes what you can learn depend heavily on where you sample rather than simply on how much.

\subsection{Step 1: a saturating model}

We keep the same question, but now assume that growth increases with nitrogen and then levels off as other resources become limiting. A standard choice is the Michaelis--Menten function:

\begin{equation}
	y_i = \frac{V_{\max}\, x_i}{K + x_i} + \varepsilon_i,
	\qquad \varepsilon_i \sim \mathrm{N}(0, \sigma)
	\label{eq:mm}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $V_{\max}$ is the asymptotic biomass, the value approached as nitrogen becomes unlimiting, and $K$ is the half-saturation constant, the nitrogen value at which expected biomass is $V_{\max}/2$.

Unlike the slope in Section~\ref{sec:linear}, these two parameters answer different biological questions. $V_{\max}$ describes the ceiling; $K$ describes how quickly that ceiling is approached. A study can pin down one without the other, or neither, and which of those happens is largely a matter of design.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# Assumed parameter values for the saturating model
	Vmax  <- 120  # asymptotic biomass (g)
	K     <-   8  # half-saturation constant: nitrogen at which y = Vmax / 2
	sigma <-  10  # residual SD (g)
	
	# The mean function
	mu_mm <- function(x, Vmax, K) Vmax * x / (K + x)
\end{lstlisting}

Equation~\ref{eq:mm} forces $y = 0$ at $x = 0$, which is unrealistic if the soil already contains nitrogen. Adding a baseline term is straightforward and makes the identifiability problem below somewhat worse rather than better; we keep the simpler form for clarity.

\subsection{Step 2: two candidate designs, same sample size}

The key design choice in a non-linear model is the range of $x$. We simulate two designs with identical sample sizes: one sampling nitrogen only in a low to moderate range, and one extending far enough to approach the asymptote.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# Two designs, same n, differing only in how far the nitrogen gradient extends
	n <- 50
	x_narrow <- runif(n, 0, 12)   # Design A: stops well short of saturation
	x_wide   <- runif(n, 0, 40)   # Design B: extends past the bend
	
	fake_narrow <- data.frame(x = x_narrow,
	y = mu_mm(x_narrow, Vmax, K) + rnorm(n, 0, sigma))
	fake_wide   <- data.frame(x = x_wide,
	y = mu_mm(x_wide,   Vmax, K) + rnorm(n, 0, sigma))
\end{lstlisting}

Plotting the two datasets shows the problem immediately (Fig.~\ref{fig:mmdesigns}). Over the narrow range the saturating curve is nearly a straight line. The data contain almost no information about the asymptote, because the design never went anywhere near it. Same $n$, same model, same noise, but Design~A cannot see the thing the model is about.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	% \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_mm_designs}
	\caption{Two designs with the same sample size ($n = 50$) under Equation~\ref{eq:mm} with $V_{\max} = 120$, $K = 8$ and $\sigma = 10$. (a) A narrow nitrogen range gives data that look almost linear and carry little information about the asymptote. (b) A wider range reveals the curvature and the onset of saturation. The solid line is the generating model in both panels.}
	\label{fig:mmdesigns}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Step 3: fitting, convergence, and the cloud of estimates}

We fit the planned model with non-linear least squares. Because \texttt{nls} can fail to converge, it is tempting to set \texttt{warnOnly = TRUE} and move on. We recommend against it: that option returns non-converged fits as though they were estimates, and quietly contaminates everything computed downstream. Recording convergence status instead turns a nuisance into a design diagnostic.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# Fit the saturating model; return NULL rather than a non-converged fit
	fit_mm <- function(dat) {
		fit <- try(nls(y ~ Vmax * x / (K + x), data = dat,
		start = list(Vmax = max(dat$y), K = median(dat$x)),
		control = nls.control(maxiter = 200)), silent = TRUE)
		if (inherits(fit, "try-error")) NULL else fit
	}
\end{lstlisting}

Under Design~A the fit fails to converge in XX\% of simulated studies, against YY\% under Design~B. That difference is a result in its own right: it says the narrow design is asking the data for something they cannot supply. A caution follows immediately. Discarding failed runs before summarising the rest is itself a selection step, of the kind this paper warns against elsewhere, since the surviving estimates are the subset that happened to be informative. How many runs were dropped should be reported alongside the estimates that remain.

Repeating the whole study many times under each design and plotting the resulting parameter estimates against one another makes the underlying problem visible (Fig.~\ref{fig:mmcloud}). Under Design~A the estimates smear along a ridge: many combinations of $K$ and $V_{\max}$ produce almost identical curves over $x \in [0, 12]$, so the data cannot tell them apart. A large $V_{\max}$ paired with a large $K$ looks much the same, over that range, as a smaller $V_{\max}$ paired with a smaller $K$. Under Design~B the cloud collapses around the true values. This is a design problem rather than an analysis problem: no cleverer statistical method recovers information that the sampling design never collected.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	% \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_mm_cloud}
	\caption{Estimates of $K$ and $V_{\max}$ from 500 simulated studies under each design, with the true values marked. (a) Under the narrow design the two parameters trade off against one another and neither is well determined. (b) Under the wide design both are recovered. Non-converged fits are excluded; their frequency is reported in the text.}
	\label{fig:mmcloud}
\end{figure}

\subsection{More data, or better placed data?}

The design question can now be put directly. Given a fixed budget, is it better to collect more observations or to spread them over a wider range of nitrogen?

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# Compare the spread of K estimates across sample sizes and nitrogen ranges
	ns <- c(20, 50, 100, 200)
	sapply(ns, function(nn) IQR(replicate_K(nn, x_max = 12)))  # narrow
	sapply(ns, function(nn) IQR(replicate_K(nn, x_max = 40)))  # wide
\end{lstlisting}

Quadrupling the sample size under the narrow design buys less than simply extending the nitrogen gradient at the original sample size (Fig.~\ref{fig:mmtradeoff}). If a project can afford one change, the simulation says which one to make, and it says so before the money is spent. This is the central practical claim of the paper in its sharpest form: identifiability and precision can depend as much on where you sample as on how many observations you collect.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	% \includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{fig_mm_n_vs_range}
	\caption{Interquartile range of estimates of $K$ against sample size, under the narrow and wide nitrogen ranges. Extending the range at $n = 50$ achieves more than quadrupling the sample size within the narrow range.}
	\label{fig:mmtradeoff}
\end{figure}

\bigskip

\noindent So far the analysis model has always matched the model that generated the data, and the design has always been experimental. Section~\ref{sec:limits} relaxes each assumption in turn, and shows that neither failure is visible in the precision of the result.

\section{When precision is not enough}
\label{sec:limits}

Sections~\ref{sec:linear} and \ref{sec:nonlinear} treated precision as the design criterion. That is deliberate, but it is not sufficient, and this section sets the bound. A 95\% interval is a statement about sampling variability under an assumed model. It says nothing about whether that model has the right structure, and nothing about whether the parameter it surrounds is the one the scientific question was about. We demonstrate each failure in turn. In both, the interval is narrow, the diagnostics are clean, and the answer is wrong.

\subsection{A model with the wrong shape}

We fit an ordinary straight line to data generated by the saturating process of Section~\ref{sec:nonlinear}, using the narrow design in which the curve looks almost linear anyway.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# Fit a straight line to data generated by the saturating model
	fit_wrong <- lm(y ~ x, data = fake_narrow)
	summary(fit_wrong)$r.squared                       # looks like a good fit
	confint(fit_wrong)["x", ]                          # a tight interval
	predict(fit_wrong, newdata = data.frame(x = 40))   # extrapolate to x = 40
	mu_mm(40, Vmax, K)                                 # what the truth would be
\end{lstlisting}

Every conventional diagnostic looks reassuring. The model explains about three-quarters of the variance, the slope is estimated to within a couple of per cent, and nothing in the output flags a problem. The predicted biomass at $x = 40$ is about 234\,g. The true value is 100\,g (Fig.~\ref{fig:limits}a). Collecting more data would tighten that interval without moving it, because the interval tightens around a value that was never the target.

There is a constructive reading. The same simulation that exposes the problem points to the remedy: a design extending past the bend both identifies the parameters (Section~\ref{sec:nonlinear}) and makes the inadequacy of a linear model obvious in the data. Designing for precision and designing to be able to detect one's own mistakes turn out, here, to be the same activity.

\subsection{A parameter that is not the one we mean}

Now suppose the model has the right shape but we cannot randomise. We survey plots across a landscape, measuring nitrogen where it happens to occur. Soil moisture varies across that landscape and affects both: wetter plots mineralise more nitrogen, and wetter plots also grow more biomass for reasons unrelated to nitrogen.

The step that matters is Step~1, and it is easy to skip. The generative model must describe how we believe the data actually arise, including processes we do not intend to measure. Writing moisture into the simulation is not a claim that we will have moisture data; it is a claim about the world.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
	# An observational design in which moisture drives both nitrogen and biomass
	n_obs    <- 500
	moisture <- rnorm(n_obs, 0, 1)                              # the confounder
	x_obs    <- 10 + 3 * moisture + rnorm(n_obs, 0, 1)          # N tracks moisture
	y_obs    <- 30 + 7 * x_obs + 15 * moisture + rnorm(n_obs, 0, 5)
	
	naive <- lm(y_obs ~ x_obs)                       # moisture unmeasured
	adj   <- lm(y_obs ~ x_obs + moisture)            # moisture measured
\end{lstlisting}

The causal effect of nitrogen is 7. The naive analysis returns about 11.5, with a 95\% interval of roughly $[11.3, 11.7]$ that excludes the truth entirely; the adjusted analysis recovers 6.9 (Fig.~\ref{fig:limits}b). Nothing in the naive output signals a problem: the residuals are well behaved, the model is correctly specified as a straight line, and the estimate is very precise.

Precision is again the difficulty. The bias does not depend on $n$, so a larger sample narrows the interval without correcting the estimate. At $n = 10\,000$ the naive interval spans 11.44 to 11.53 (Fig.~\ref{fig:limits}c). A large dataset, analysed with a correctly specified model, yields an extremely confident answer to a question nobody asked.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	% \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_limits}
	\caption{Two ways a precise design can mislead. (a) A straight line fitted to data generated by the saturating model over a narrow nitrogen range (shaded): the fit is good by conventional criteria, yet extrapolating to $x = 40$ overpredicts biomass by more than a factor of two. (b) Estimates of the nitrogen effect at $n = 500$ from an analysis omitting soil moisture (naive) and one including it (adjusted); the dashed line is the true causal effect of 7. (c) The naive estimate as sample size increases from 100 to 10\,000: the interval contracts steadily around a value roughly 65\% too large.}
	\label{fig:limits}
\end{figure}

\subsection{What follows for the loop}

The simulation loop does not solve either problem. It cannot tell you that your functional form is wrong, and no amount of simulation licenses a causal claim from an observational design; that requires randomisation or an explicit argument about which variables must be measured and adjusted for \citep{pearlSevenToolsCausal2019, graceScientistsGuideDeveloping2020, arifApplyingStructuralCausal2023}.

What it offers is earlier warning, and three things follow. It quantifies the damage while it can still be acted on: simulating a confounder one suspects exists takes a few lines and answers a concrete question, and here the answer is 65\%, large enough to change what one would do. It disciplines the estimand, because the naive analysis above is a perfectly good estimate of an association and wrong only as an answer to a causal question, a distinction that writing the generative model forces into the open. And it corrects a natural intuition about sample size: it is tempting to treat a large dataset as insurance against error, whereas for this class of problem the larger study is the more confidently wrong one, and the less likely to be questioned.

This is why we treat Step~1 as the step that repays most attention, and the one most often skipped. The precision machinery of Sections~\ref{sec:linear} and \ref{sec:nonlinear} is only as good as the generative model it is conditioned on.

\section{Reporting a simulation loop}
\label{sec:reporting}

The loop is only useful to a reader if they can disagree with it productively. That requires reporting the assumptions, not just the resulting design. The checklist below lists what we think a paper or proposal should state; all of it fits comfortably in a short methods paragraph plus a supplement.

The same three steps extend without modification to two-group comparisons, generalised responses such as counts and proportions, and hierarchical or repeated-measures designs; only the generative model and the analysis model change. The Supplement works through a nested design in which a fixed budget of plots is allocated across sites in five different ways, a case where no closed-form guidance exists and the differences in precision are substantial.

\begin{mytextbox}[title=\textbf{Checklist: what to report from a simulation loop}]
	\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=0.4em, topsep=0.4em]
		
		\item \textbf{The generative model.} Equations for the mean function $f(x; \theta)$ and for the stochastic component $\varepsilon$, on the response scale.
		
		\item \textbf{The assumed parameter values,} with their justification: literature, pilot data, or expert judgement labelled as such. State the range explored where a single value could not be defended.
		
		\item \textbf{What the error term represents:} process variation, measurement error, or both, and whether hierarchy or autocorrelation is included.
		
		\item \textbf{The design variables explored:} sample size, treatment levels, predictor range, replication structure, measurement effort. Say which were varied and which were held fixed.
		
		\item \textbf{The performance criterion,} and the target. We recommend the width of the uncertainty interval for the focal parameter, summarised at a stated quantile rather than as a mean, together with the scientific or management requirement the target derives from.
		
		\item \textbf{Failures as well as successes:} the proportion of simulated fits that did not converge, and how they were handled. This is a design diagnostic, not a nuisance.
		
		\item \textbf{Reproducibility:} the code, the random seed, and software versions, archived with a persistent identifier.
		
	\end{enumerate}
\end{mytextbox}

\section{Outlook}
\label{sec:outlook}

The proposal of this paper is narrow. Turn a verbal hypothesis into an explicit generative model, simulate what it implies, and choose a design by the precision it delivers for the parameter that matters. Everything else follows from taking that seriously, including the uncomfortable findings of Section~\ref{sec:limits}.

Advice of this kind is not new, and it is worth asking why it has not been taken up more widely. We think the honest answer is that it was expensive. Writing a generative model for anything beyond a straight line has required a level of comfort with code that ecological training has mostly reserved for theoreticians, and an empiricist facing a field season has had good reason to skip it. That barrier is now falling quickly. Code assistants make a first simulation of a nested, non-Gaussian or saturating design something that can be drafted in minutes rather than an afternoon, and revised as easily. The practical case for the loop is therefore stronger today than when much of the same advice was offered a decade ago, not because the argument has improved but because the cost of acting on it has dropped.

What remains expensive is the part that matters most. A model that can be simulated from still has to be defended: which processes generate the data, what magnitudes are plausible, what varies and by how much, and what the study is actually trying to estimate. Those judgements are ecological rather than computational, and nothing about cheaper code supplies them. If anything the balance shifts further towards Step~1, because the steps that used to consume the effort no longer do. The same is true of the design target itself. Choosing an interval width of $2k$ is a statement about how much error is tolerable for the claim at stake, which is a scientific and sometimes a policy judgement rather than a statistical one \citep{douglasInductiveRiskValues2000, steelEpistemicValuesArgument2010}. Making the generative model explicit does not settle such questions, but it turns them from hidden defaults into inspectable choices \citep{romeijnPhilosophyStatistics2014, gelmanPhilosophyPracticeBayesian2013}, which is the most that can be asked of any method \citep{boxScienceStatistics1976}.

These considerations become more pressing, not less, as ecological analyses draw on larger datasets and on AI-assisted pattern discovery. Modern methods are very good at finding regularities in high-dimensional data and comparatively poor at telling us which of them to believe \citep{pichlerMachineLearningDeep2023, lucasTranslucentBoxInterpretable2020}. Simulation offers one concrete form of discipline here: a generative model specifies what the data would look like if a proposed mechanism were absent, and a pattern that a method also recovers from such data has not been established by finding it. The confounded example of Section~\ref{sec:limits} is the more sobering case, because it scales in the wrong direction. Larger samples narrowed the interval without moving the estimate, so the studies that command most confidence are the ones whose bias is hardest to notice. Volume of data is not a substitute for having asked what generated it \citep{pearlSevenToolsCausal2019}, and reporting standards for uncertainty in ecology remain uneven enough that the difference is easy to miss \citep{simmondsRecommendationsQuantitativeUncertainty2024, leleHowShouldWe2020}.

None of this requires a new methodology. It requires doing something ordinary at an unusual time, namely writing down what one expects before finding out, and it costs an afternoon at the point in a project where an afternoon is cheapest. We have found the exercise most valuable when it went badly: when the simulated data looked nothing like the system, when the estimates would not settle however much data we imagined collecting, or when the design we had planned turned out to answer a question adjacent to the one we cared about. Those are useful things to learn in front of a computer, and expensive things to learn in a field.

	\newpage


\bibliography{Art_simulation}
\bibliographystyle{ecolett}


	\newpage
\nolinenumbers

\begin{tcolorbox}[
	title=Box 1: Nine pitfalls along the research workflow,
	colback=black!2,
	colframe=black!35,
	fonttitle=\bfseries,
	boxrule=0.6pt,
	arc=2mm,
	breakable,
	left=2mm,right=2mm,top=1mm,bottom=1mm
	]
	
	\textit{Numbered as in Figure~\ref{fig:workflow}, in the order they arise along the workflow.} $\bullet$~\textit{marks pitfalls a design choice can fix, and which the simulation loop therefore addresses directly;} $\circ$~\textit{those it can expose or quantify but not resolve.}
	
	\vspace{5pt}
	\textbf{Framing the question and hypothesis}
	
	$\circ$~\textbf{(1) Estimating something other than the quantity of interest.} An association is reported as though it were a causal effect, because a driver of both the predictor and the response was unmeasured, or because sampling was selective. The estimate may be perfectly good as a description and still answer a different question from the one asked. Refs: \citealt{pearlSevenToolsCausal2019, graceScientistsGuideDeveloping2020, arifApplyingStructuralCausal2023}.
	
	$\circ$~\textbf{(2) Decisions made after seeing the data.} Hypotheses adjusted to fit results, outcomes or subsets chosen once their behaviour is known, and analyses continued until they look right. This rarely feels like misconduct, because each individual choice is defensible; what is not defensible is that the choices were available. Refs: \citealt{gelmanGardenForkingPaths2013, kerrHARKingHypothesizingResults1998, stefanBigLittleLies2023, nickersonConfirmationBiasUbiquitous1998}.
	
	$\bullet$~\textbf{(3) A hypothesis that cannot be wrong.} The claim has no stated direction, no expected magnitude, and no defined alternative, so almost any result can be read as support. Vagueness survives argument but not arithmetic: a hypothesis that cannot be written as a formula with numbers in it was never specific enough to test. Refs: \citealt{rajtmajerHowFailureFalsify2022, gigerenzerMindlessStatistics2004, perezgonzalezFisherNeymanPearsonNHST2015}.
	
	\vspace{5pt}
	\textbf{Designing the study}
	
	$\bullet$~\textbf{(4) Designing to detect rather than to estimate.} Sample size chosen so that a test is likely to cross a significance threshold, rather than so that the quantity of interest will be known precisely enough to support the claim at stake. The two questions have different answers, and the second is the one a reader needs. Refs: \citealt{goodmanUsePredictedConfidence1994, rothmanPlanningStudySize2018, hoenigAbusePowerPervasive2001, halseyFickleValueGenerates2015}.
	
	$\bullet$~\textbf{(5) Sampling that cannot identify the target.} Predictor values spanning too narrow a range, treatment levels placed where the response is flat, or too few groups to estimate a group-varying effect. Where the observations are taken can matter more than how many there are, and no increase in sample size compensates for a design that never visits the conditions the parameter describes. Refs: \citealt{bolkerEcologicalModelsData2008, direnzoPracticalGuideUnderstanding2023}; this paper, Section~\ref{sec:nonlinear}.
	
	$\bullet$~\textbf{(6) Ignoring how the data actually arise.} Measurement error, imperfect detection, misclassification, and observations that are not independent because they share a plot, a site, or a time. Unlike variation in the system itself, much of this is a design choice: better instruments, repeated readings, or a different allocation of effort. Refs: \citealt{direnzoPracticalGuideUnderstanding2023, popovicFourPrinciplesImproved2024}.
	
	\vspace{5pt}
	\textbf{Analysing and reporting}
	
	$\circ$~\textbf{(7) A model that does not match the process.} A saturating response fitted as a straight line, the wrong distribution or link, omitted grouping structure, a transformation that changes what the parameters mean, or extrapolation beyond the range sampled. Such a model can fit well, report a narrow interval, and still be badly wrong about the quantity that matters; regression to the mean is a familiar special case. Refs: \citealt{direnzoPracticalGuideUnderstanding2023, popovicFourPrinciplesImproved2024, barnettRegressionMeanWhat2005, wolkovichSimpleExplanationDeclining2021a}.
	
	$\bullet$~\textbf{(8) Conclusions that turn on a threshold.} Results summarised as present or absent rather than as a magnitude with uncertainty, and non-significant findings read as evidence of no effect when they are usually compatible with effects large enough to matter. Reporting the interval instead removes the problem at source. Refs: \citealt{amrheinEarthFlat0052017, amrheinAbandoningStatisticalSignificance2019, wassersteinMovingWorld052019, muffRewritingResultsSections2022, singalPrenatalAntidepressantUse2017a}.
	
	$\circ$~\textbf{(9) The significance filter on the literature.} Because significant results are more likely to be written up and published, the published subset of an imprecise design overstates the effect and sometimes reverses its sign, even when every individual study was analysed correctly. The exaggeration is invisible from inside any one study but computable from the design in advance. Refs: \citealt{gelmanPowerCalculationsAssessing2014, vanzwetSignificanceFilterWinners2021, kimmelEmpiricalEvidenceWidespread2023, yangPublicationBiasImpacts2023, ioannidisWhyMostPublished2005a}.
	
\end{tcolorbox}
\linenumbers
	\newpage

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{workflow}
	\caption{The simulation loop within the conventional research workflow. The lower row shows the familiar sequence from research question to conclusion, with a dashed return path indicating that conclusions raise new questions. The simulation loop (upper panel) is inserted between forming a hypothesis and collecting data, and runs entirely before any observation is made. Its three steps are to translate the hypothesis into an explicit generative model, to simulate and plot the data that model implies, and to iterate the design against a target precision for the parameter of interest. Arrows within the loop run in both directions: simulated data that look implausible send one back to the model, and a design that cannot deliver useful precision sends one back to the question. Numbered markers show the stage at which each pitfall of Box~1 arises. Filled markers ($\bullet$) are pitfalls a design choice can fix, and which the loop therefore addresses directly; open markers ($\circ$) are those it can expose or quantify but not resolve. The loop is placed where it is because sample size, treatment levels, predictor range and measurement effort together fix what a study can reveal, and no later choice of analysis recovers information the design did not collect.}
	\label{fig:workflow}
\end{figure}



